WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:20.086
As I mentioned last
Friday, there is one more topic

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:23.456
that I want to cover.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:26.092
And that is the
topic of diffusion.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:31.965
And so today, I'm going to
talk about diffusion, which

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:37.470
will be in the context of
what diffusion depends on

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.973
and how do we describe it.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:41.674
And in particular,
there's two laws,

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.777
Fick's first law and
Fick's second law,

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:44.911
that I want to talk about.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.846
And I want to talk
about how this

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.848
looks in the context of
some of the chemistry

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.551
that we've learned, some of
the things that we've learned.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.921
So diffusion, this is
kind of a standard picture

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.157
people show for diffusion,
although really, this

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:02.462
is probably mostly convection,
which is the movement.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:05.397
It's not diffusion.

00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:09.269
But diffusion kind of has
taken on this broader meaning.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.138
And so what do we
mean by diffusion?

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:18.912
Well, diffusion comes from
the Latin word diffundere.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.381
And that means to spread out.

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:30.289
But in this class,
we're going to start

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:35.962
by thinking about it as
a particular movement

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.564
down a concentration gradient.

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.265
And that is what's
happening here.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.202
You see there's a very
concentrated droplet

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:43.402
of dye that you put in there.

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:46.239
And then it's less
concentrated out here.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.607
You probably don't have any.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:49.676
So the concentration of
the dye out there is zero.

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:51.444
And in here it's very high.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:56.348
And so it wants to go down
a concentration gradient.

00:01:56.348 --> 00:02:03.556
So that's movement down
a concentration gradient.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:10.896
OK.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.069
How do we describe
this movement?

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:21.207
And it really goes
back to experiments

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.742
that Robert Brown did.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:26.679
And this is the original
paper that he wrote way back

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:29.048
in the early 1800s.

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:32.352
I love the title of this
paper, "A Brief Account

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:36.289
of Microscopical Observations
Made Specifically

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.659
in the Months of June, July,
and August 1827 on the Particles

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.594
Contained in the
Pollen and Plants

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.597
and on the General Existence
of Active Molecules in Organic

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.699
and Inorganic Bodies."

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:50.970
What a nice, detailed title.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.339
That was Brown.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:56.609
So he started with pollen.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.477
And he looked at it.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:58.478
And it looked like this.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.146
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.281
Oh, that's very loud.

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:09.189
There wasn't
supposed to be music.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:12.458
So that's what he saw, except
this isn't what he saw.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:15.929
This is what Koshu Endo
randomly posted online

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.664
and I'm showing you.

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:20.133
But if you look at
pollen in a microscope,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.735
and you're Robert Brown
back in the early 1800s,

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.837
you'd see this.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:28.541
So he thought, aha,
OK, there's life.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:31.844
This is must have
to do with biology.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.112
And things are alive.

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.314
And so they move.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.249
And it must be
something like that.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:38.284
And so he put other things in.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:41.988
And they did the same
thing, they jiggled around.

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:43.856
And then he said,
well, OK, what about

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:48.328
if I put something that was once
alive but is not alive anymore.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.196
And it did the same thing.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.331
He said, OK, wait a second.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:54.701
Maybe there's something to do
with having ever been alive,

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:57.503
the spirit of the particles.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.239
And he said, OK, the
ultimate spirit, I'm

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.707
going to break some glass--

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:03.576
which is what he
did, I love that.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.978
I like breaking glass too.

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:06.512
And so he broke the glass.

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.047
And he took little
pieces of glass

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:09.382
and put it under his microscope.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.383
They did the same thing.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.353
They all did the same thing.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:15.088
So they were really perplexed.

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.223
What was going on?

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:20.125
This is the microscopic basis.

00:04:20.125 --> 00:04:22.295
This is now called
Brownian motion.

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:28.635
It's random motion due to
thermal energy and collisions.

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:34.440
And it's the connection of
this microscopic random motion

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.878
due to thermal energy and
macroscopic observables that

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:44.384
led to a big advance in our
understanding of diffusion.

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:49.589
And so the question of
how far took 80 years.

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:51.124
So one of the first
questions, you

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:53.558
say, how far do these things go.

00:04:53.558 --> 00:04:54.527
How far are they going?

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:55.695
They're randomly moving around.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:56.195
I got that.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.530
I see it in the microscope.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.165
How far do they go?

00:04:59.165 --> 00:04:59.832
So how far?

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:06.673
Well, that was a
question that you

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:13.646
can answer if you can get the
mean squared displacement.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:14.180
I don't know.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.481
I say tomato.

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:24.724
And so displacement, it's OK,
mean squared displacement.

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:32.031
And it really came
from Einstein in 1905.

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:35.468
Actually, this is what Einstein
did for his dissertation,

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:37.770
his Ph.D. work
was on this topic.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.640
It was on
understanding how to go

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.745
from these kind of microscopic
random fluctuations

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.881
to some macroscopic observables.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:51.084
And so, the mean squared
displacement, well,

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:57.090
that's just the average of some
measurement of some distance

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.224
the particles go.

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:02.261
So if you go back to
the dye, there it is.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:05.064
But now it's in atom
form or molecule form.

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:07.033
And it's diffusing
around, diffusing around.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:10.670
And OK, you say, well,
how far did it go.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.839
That would be the circle.

00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:16.642
And that's something that
Einstein tackled in his Ph.D.

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:20.947
and found that the mean
squared displacement is--

00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:26.152
well, let's just say it's equal
to 6 times some constant times

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.488
time.

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:33.092
This is in three dimensions.

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:35.728
If you're in two
dimensions, it's 4.

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:38.865
If you're in one
dimension, it's 2.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:40.133
But the main point isn't that.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:43.636
The main point is that the
mean squared displacement

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:48.174
is a function of some
constant times time.

00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:50.443
And so if you just
wait long enough

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:52.812
and you know this
constant, then you

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:55.114
can figure out how
far things diffuse.

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.150
That was pretty cool.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.017
That was pretty cool.

00:06:58.017 --> 00:06:59.986
But you see, now,
what we're going

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:04.457
to be more interested in
in this class is not how

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:06.692
far but how fast.

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:09.896
We're interested in how fast.

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:14.901
And again, these are questions
that Brown couldn't answer.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.870
Brown observed these things
and wrote about them.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.639
And that's why it's
called Brownian motion.

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:22.141
But it was later that
people said, well,

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.110
let's try to think about
how to write this down

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.380
and come up with
theories that explain it.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:31.784
And so for how fast,
now we have the flux.

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:38.191
So we have the flux or
the diffusion flux J.

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:42.695
And what we want is a way
of describing how much.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:52.572
So it's an amount of
something, the amount

00:07:52.572 --> 00:07:54.440
of the substance per area.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.641
You're going to normalize it.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:57.743
So you say, I'm going
to take this sheet.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:07:59.245
And I'm going to
say, how much of it

00:07:59.245 --> 00:08:03.950
is crossing this
sheet area per time.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:18.030
So amount of a substance per
area per time, that's the flux.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.434
So it could be grams.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:26.873
The amount could be grams,
mass, kilograms, micrograms,

00:08:26.873 --> 00:08:28.841
nanograms.

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:32.544
It could be number
of atoms, moles.

00:08:32.544 --> 00:08:34.447
That's also an amount, moles.

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:36.849
So we just say
amount, it's general.

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:45.958
Grams, moles, et cetera,
n number of atoms.

00:08:45.958 --> 00:08:47.960
That's what moles tells us.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.495
Grams, et cetera--

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:50.963
The time could also be--

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:52.999
well, the area could
be centimeter squared,

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:53.933
meters squared.

00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:54.867
That's an area.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:57.003
And then the time
is usually seconds.

00:08:57.003 --> 00:09:00.172
Usually, it doesn't have to be.

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:02.975
And so this is what
Fick's law gives us.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:07.680
So this was first established.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.983
How do you get a
dependence of this flux?

00:09:10.983 --> 00:09:12.685
How do you get a
dependence of this flux?

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:14.954
And again, we've got
this constant in there.

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:18.424
And Fick's first law
is all about that.

00:09:18.424 --> 00:09:26.065
Fick's first law says that
this flux is proportional to--

00:09:26.065 --> 00:09:28.134
and I'm just going to write
it like this for now--

00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:32.772
concentration gradient.

00:09:37.543 --> 00:09:43.583
So that's C divided
by the change in X. C

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:45.351
is concentration here.

00:09:45.351 --> 00:09:47.386
We'll draw an arrow.

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:49.288
Concentration is C.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:51.591
Now, we could use brackets.

00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:55.027
Bracket A, concentration
of A, mass per liter,

00:09:55.027 --> 00:09:56.529
something like that.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:10:00.533
I'll use the letter C,
delta C over delta X.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:02.068
So that's the
concentration gradient.

00:10:05.538 --> 00:10:08.341
Oh, by the way
this wasn't as far.

00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:09.809
It didn't take as
long as Einstein.

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:15.448
But this was 1855.

00:10:15.448 --> 00:10:17.650
OK, good.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.853
So Fick came along
maybe 30 years later.

00:10:20.853 --> 00:10:23.356
And he said, well, I want to
know how fast things are going.

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:25.758
I want to understand
how to describe

00:10:25.758 --> 00:10:30.096
the flux of these
randomly moving things.

00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:34.133
And I notice that it depends on
the concentration difference.

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:40.106
And in particular, I like it
when diffusion is positive.

00:10:40.106 --> 00:10:45.444
So J is equal to
minus D times dc/dx.

00:10:45.444 --> 00:10:47.880
And that is Fick's first law.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.949
Gesundheit.

00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:50.915
And D is a constant.

00:10:50.915 --> 00:10:52.018
D is this constant again.

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:54.153
So D is the diffusion constant.

00:10:54.153 --> 00:10:58.357
We'll talk about this,
diffusion constant.

00:11:01.093 --> 00:11:02.962
There's the
concentration gradient.

00:11:02.962 --> 00:11:05.931
And we got a minus sign.

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:10.836
Notice, movement down a
concentration gradient.

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:12.271
But if I didn't
have a minus sign,

00:11:12.271 --> 00:11:14.440
then I wouldn't have
a positive flux.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.443
Then the droplet
wouldn't spread out.

00:11:17.443 --> 00:11:19.245
That wouldn't be good.

00:11:19.245 --> 00:11:21.414
So we're going from
high concentration

00:11:21.414 --> 00:11:22.548
to low concentration.

00:11:22.548 --> 00:11:25.384
When I've got a bigger
change in the concentration,

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:27.119
I've got faster diffusion.

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:29.455
That's what Fick's
first law tells us.

00:11:29.455 --> 00:11:31.924
OK, good.

00:11:31.924 --> 00:11:38.097
Very importantly, Fick's first
law means it's steady state.

00:11:38.097 --> 00:11:41.367
Notice, there is no t here.

00:11:41.367 --> 00:11:43.469
There's no time.

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:45.438
For that, we have to
wait for the second law.

00:11:45.438 --> 00:11:51.744
So steady state
diffusion, and that

00:11:51.744 --> 00:11:56.215
means not a function of time.

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:07.226
The kinds of problems where
Fick's first law matters is--

00:12:07.226 --> 00:12:09.695
and I know some of you have
probably seen Fick's first law.

00:12:09.695 --> 00:12:10.196
I get that.

00:12:10.196 --> 00:12:15.267
But in a little bit, we're
going to connect it to crystals.

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:19.338
But the kind of diffusion
where the first floor matters

00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:23.943
is the kind where you're
holding concentrations equal.

00:12:23.943 --> 00:12:26.846
Let's say you're holding
a concentration gradient,

00:12:26.846 --> 00:12:29.315
and it's not changing.

00:12:29.315 --> 00:12:31.751
And so you're
wondering, well, if I

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:35.020
hold a concentration at one
thing here and at another thing

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.690
here, how fast do
things diffuse.

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:39.992
That's what Fick's law tells us.

00:12:39.992 --> 00:12:43.195
And so let's do an example.

00:12:43.195 --> 00:12:43.996
Here's my example.

00:12:43.996 --> 00:12:50.336
I found these gloves, because
I was looking for copolymers.

00:12:50.336 --> 00:12:55.274
Here's a copolymer that doesn't
have the nitrile groups.

00:12:55.274 --> 00:12:57.409
Remember, the nitrile
groups last Friday

00:12:57.409 --> 00:13:00.279
that were so important when we
were doing our polymer design.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.081
This doesn't have it.

00:13:02.081 --> 00:13:03.649
This is butyl rubber.

00:13:03.649 --> 00:13:04.250
OK, fine.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.684
So there's the copolymer.

00:13:05.684 --> 00:13:08.420
It's copolymerized
with these two groups.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:12.725
But now, it's got those
groups, because it gave me

00:13:12.725 --> 00:13:15.461
the right flexibility and
some of the other factors

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:16.862
that I want in the glove.

00:13:16.862 --> 00:13:19.665
But is it strong
enough chemically?

00:13:19.665 --> 00:13:21.967
And that's a very
important question.

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:26.539
We talked about this last Friday
that these chemical groups

00:13:26.539 --> 00:13:27.907
change the mechanical
properties.

00:13:27.907 --> 00:13:31.343
They also use the
chemical resilience.

00:13:31.343 --> 00:13:32.845
And so here I have it.

00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:34.680
I have a toxin.

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.982
This is why we wear
gloves, because we

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:42.154
don't want to get methyl
chloride into our skin.

00:13:42.154 --> 00:13:45.925
Methyl chloride is toxic
at 50 parts per million.

00:13:45.925 --> 00:13:47.993
Keep it out.

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:49.361
Be very careful.

00:13:49.361 --> 00:13:51.197
So I don't know.

00:13:51.197 --> 00:13:54.133
I look up online and say,
well, OK, butyl rubber.

00:13:54.133 --> 00:13:55.201
These feel comfortable.

00:13:55.201 --> 00:13:56.135
But how are they?

00:13:56.135 --> 00:13:59.905
OK, with methyl chloride, which
is a toxin, it's a molecule,

00:13:59.905 --> 00:14:02.708
it has a diffusion
constant of 1 times 10

00:14:02.708 --> 00:14:05.144
to the minus 6 centimeters
squared per second.

00:14:05.144 --> 00:14:08.581
The gloves are 0.04
centimeters thick.

00:14:08.581 --> 00:14:11.717
And I'm working
with paint remover.

00:14:11.717 --> 00:14:12.952
I'm working with paint room.

00:14:12.952 --> 00:14:14.353
The concentration
of paint remover

00:14:14.353 --> 00:14:16.455
is a gram per centimeter cubed.

00:14:16.455 --> 00:14:17.990
Am I safe?

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:21.260
How much am I going
to be exposed to this?

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.429
At my skin, I'm
starting out with zero.

00:14:23.429 --> 00:14:25.464
What's the flux
through the glove?

00:14:25.464 --> 00:14:26.565
Can I stay safe?

00:14:26.565 --> 00:14:30.469
Well, first of all, one
thing that we can do

00:14:30.469 --> 00:14:31.971
is we can look at--

00:14:31.971 --> 00:14:33.305
just to make sure we understand.

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:35.841
So there's the
diffusion constant.

00:14:35.841 --> 00:14:39.044
Why does it have those units?

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:40.379
You can see this.

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:47.453
The units of J are an amount--

00:14:47.453 --> 00:14:49.288
this is what we already said--

00:14:49.288 --> 00:14:55.361
divided by an area times a time.

00:14:55.361 --> 00:15:01.300
So if the units of
C are an amount--

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.402
OK, I stayed with grams--

00:15:03.402 --> 00:15:10.242
over a volume, then you can
see right away that diffusion

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:18.350
must be area over time.

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:21.320
It has to be.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:26.325
If it's not, then
the units don't work.

00:15:26.325 --> 00:15:28.861
I've got concentration
over a length.

00:15:28.861 --> 00:15:32.765
This is a 1 D. I'm going
across some membrane

00:15:32.765 --> 00:15:35.634
maybe or some area.

00:15:35.634 --> 00:15:38.704
This is a membrane, the glove.

00:15:38.704 --> 00:15:40.973
And so you can think
about this for yourself.

00:15:40.973 --> 00:15:45.678
The units have to
be area over time.

00:15:45.678 --> 00:15:47.846
Is it area over time?

00:15:47.846 --> 00:15:49.982
Centimeters squared per second.

00:15:49.982 --> 00:15:51.016
They were, OK.

00:15:51.016 --> 00:15:53.052
So that worked.

00:15:53.052 --> 00:15:59.758
Now, another thing is,
before we do the math,

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:02.728
why does this happen anyway?

00:16:02.728 --> 00:16:04.863
Why is there diffusion?

00:16:04.863 --> 00:16:09.635
And it's actually quite
straightforward to understand,

00:16:09.635 --> 00:16:13.839
even when you go all the way
back to Brown's experiments

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:14.540
with pollen.

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:17.509
You say, well, things
are moving randomly.

00:16:17.509 --> 00:16:20.012
Why is there now a direction?

00:16:20.012 --> 00:16:21.747
Why is there now a direction?

00:16:21.747 --> 00:16:22.715
How is that possible?

00:16:22.715 --> 00:16:28.587
How can there be a direction
if it's all random?

00:16:28.587 --> 00:16:30.622
How can there be a
concentration gradient?

00:16:30.622 --> 00:16:32.558
And then you can get
that, because if you

00:16:32.558 --> 00:16:34.560
have some high concentration--

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:39.331
Let's suppose that this
is a high concentration,

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:42.368
and this is a low concentration.

00:16:42.368 --> 00:16:46.138
And you've got the
Brownian stuff going on.

00:16:46.138 --> 00:16:49.942
It's all in something.

00:16:49.942 --> 00:16:51.243
There's Brownian motion.

00:16:51.243 --> 00:16:54.646
Whatever containers these
are, whatever is in whatever,

00:16:54.646 --> 00:16:56.949
it's got Brownian motion.

00:16:56.949 --> 00:16:59.485
And so that means that,
because it's random,

00:16:59.485 --> 00:17:02.688
it's equally likely to go that
way as it is to go that way.

00:17:06.058 --> 00:17:07.826
But see, at the
high concentration,

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:11.096
I've got more particles.

00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:13.799
You can see that, because I
drew bigger, longer arrows,

00:17:13.799 --> 00:17:17.236
so it's obvious, more
particles, because it's

00:17:17.236 --> 00:17:18.103
more concentration.

00:17:18.103 --> 00:17:19.438
There's just a lot more of them.

00:17:19.438 --> 00:17:22.608
So there's more going across.

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:27.246
If I tried to draw a boundary
here or boundaries inside here,

00:17:27.246 --> 00:17:29.615
and I look at those as my areas.

00:17:29.615 --> 00:17:30.983
There's a high concentration.

00:17:30.983 --> 00:17:32.551
There's a lot of
stuff going randomly

00:17:32.551 --> 00:17:33.685
back and forth across them.

00:17:33.685 --> 00:17:36.722
Down here, you can
do the same thing.

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:38.157
But there's a lower
concentration,

00:17:38.157 --> 00:17:39.925
so there's less stuff
going back and forth

00:17:39.925 --> 00:17:41.260
across those barriers.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.762
And now I bring them together.

00:17:43.762 --> 00:17:44.963
I bring them together.

00:17:44.963 --> 00:17:52.771
I bring the methyl chloride
to the glove, to my hand.

00:17:52.771 --> 00:17:56.341
Or I bring something with a high
concentration next to something

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:57.743
with a low concentration.

00:17:57.743 --> 00:18:02.381
And I keep the concentrations
of the endpoints fixed.

00:18:02.381 --> 00:18:03.949
I hold these fixed.

00:18:03.949 --> 00:18:06.251
Fick's law tells me
now how this happens.

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:09.521
And you know that if I bring
them together now, well, look,

00:18:09.521 --> 00:18:12.024
this was going just a
little bit back and forth.

00:18:12.024 --> 00:18:14.193
And this was going a
lot bit back and forth.

00:18:14.193 --> 00:18:18.197
And so there's going to be
a net diffusion that way.

00:18:18.197 --> 00:18:20.365
Right there at the
edge, you can see it.

00:18:20.365 --> 00:18:24.103
More goes to the right
than goes to the left.

00:18:24.103 --> 00:18:28.774
And so this starts to
create a diffusion gradient.

00:18:28.774 --> 00:18:30.275
That'll create a
diffusion gradient.

00:18:33.412 --> 00:18:34.379
So that's why it works.

00:18:34.379 --> 00:18:38.050
That's why concentration
gradients cause diffusion.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:41.753
And Fick's first
law tells us how.

00:18:41.753 --> 00:18:44.857
OK, well, if we go
to the question--

00:18:44.857 --> 00:18:46.959
so let's see-- so the J--

00:18:46.959 --> 00:18:49.528
so now I've got my glove.

00:18:49.528 --> 00:18:56.235
And so the J is equal
minus 1.1 times 10

00:18:56.235 --> 00:19:02.207
to the minus 6 centimeters
squared per second times--

00:19:02.207 --> 00:19:04.143
and this is going to be--

00:19:04.143 --> 00:19:07.146
OK, I started with what?

00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:11.049
I started with 1 gram
right per centimeter cubed.

00:19:11.049 --> 00:19:14.553
And on the skin I've got 0.

00:19:14.553 --> 00:19:19.758
So it's going to be time 0 minus
1 gram per centimeter cubed.

00:19:19.758 --> 00:19:24.163
And that's going to be
divided by the distance 0.04

00:19:24.163 --> 00:19:26.064
centimeters.

00:19:26.064 --> 00:19:34.606
And this goes to something
like 2.5 times 10

00:19:34.606 --> 00:19:41.513
to the minus 5 grams per
centimeter squared second.

00:19:41.513 --> 00:19:42.881
That's the flux.

00:19:42.881 --> 00:19:45.684
That's what Fick's law tells us,
because my skin is good enough.

00:19:45.684 --> 00:19:48.654
My body is good enough that any
time a toxin, a methyl chloride

00:19:48.654 --> 00:19:51.990
molecule touches it,
the skin just takes it

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:56.361
away and poisons me.

00:19:56.361 --> 00:19:57.863
But that's important.

00:19:57.863 --> 00:20:00.265
It's not good, but
it's important,

00:20:00.265 --> 00:20:04.336
because it keeps this
concentration fixed.

00:20:04.336 --> 00:20:06.338
Otherwise, this would build up.

00:20:06.338 --> 00:20:08.140
And it would be changing.

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:10.742
So you can't use Fick's law.

00:20:10.742 --> 00:20:11.243
No.

00:20:11.243 --> 00:20:15.280
Fick's law applies when these
two concentrations are fixed,

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.749
and there's not a
time dependence.

00:20:17.749 --> 00:20:19.117
Instead, there's a rate.

00:20:19.117 --> 00:20:23.455
There's a flux that
you're after, J.

00:20:23.455 --> 00:20:26.592
And so now you say,
well, can I handle this.

00:20:26.592 --> 00:20:27.659
Can I handle this?

00:20:27.659 --> 00:20:31.430
Work it out, 50
parts per million.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:33.131
I should have used the nitrile.

00:20:33.131 --> 00:20:35.167
Should have used the
nitrile copolymer.

00:20:35.167 --> 00:20:37.436
Go back and make new polymers.

00:20:37.436 --> 00:20:40.906
Maybe you need much, much
better chemical resilience,

00:20:40.906 --> 00:20:45.210
which might lower this
diffusion constant.

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:47.913
This is serious stuff.

00:20:47.913 --> 00:20:49.348
This is serious stuff.

00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:51.516
And it's so serious that
I want to talk about eggs.

00:20:56.888 --> 00:21:00.125
The rubber glove
stops this toxin.

00:21:00.125 --> 00:21:01.627
Well, it slowed it down.

00:21:01.627 --> 00:21:04.029
Did it slow down enough?

00:21:04.029 --> 00:21:05.764
Use Fick's first law.

00:21:05.764 --> 00:21:10.102
But you can make membranes that
are actually very selective.

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:11.136
It's the coolest thing.

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:14.039
You make a membrane
that only allows water

00:21:14.039 --> 00:21:21.146
to go through but not salt.
That's a selective membrane.

00:21:21.146 --> 00:21:22.914
And that's called
osmosis when you can

00:21:22.914 --> 00:21:25.550
allow one fluid to go through.

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:28.453
It's semi-permeable,
permeable to one type of fluid

00:21:28.453 --> 00:21:29.121
but not another.

00:21:29.121 --> 00:21:30.689
And the egg is such
a great example,

00:21:30.689 --> 00:21:31.890
I had to show you this video.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:35.861
I made this years
ago in another class,

00:21:35.861 --> 00:21:37.696
because I wanted to show it.

00:21:37.696 --> 00:21:39.398
This is what you
can do with eggs.

00:21:39.398 --> 00:21:40.866
They're so cool.

00:21:40.866 --> 00:21:41.500
Here's a video.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:42.734
So you soak them in vinegar.

00:21:42.734 --> 00:21:45.137
OK, it's even got
directions and everything.

00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:46.471
We don't need any volume.

00:21:46.471 --> 00:21:47.239
And there they are.

00:21:47.239 --> 00:21:48.473
OK, you soak them in vinegar.

00:21:48.473 --> 00:21:50.842
Now, the vinegar dissolves
the calcium carbonate.

00:21:50.842 --> 00:21:52.444
You know this.

00:21:52.444 --> 00:21:54.613
You've done these reactions.

00:21:54.613 --> 00:21:55.280
So there you go.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:56.214
You soak them in vinegar.

00:21:56.214 --> 00:21:58.083
Leave it overnight, I'd
say, is probably best.

00:21:58.083 --> 00:21:59.318
Then you peel off the vinegar.

00:21:59.318 --> 00:22:01.286
And look, there's a membrane.

00:22:01.286 --> 00:22:03.088
The egg has a really
cool membrane.

00:22:03.088 --> 00:22:03.855
This isn't cooked.

00:22:03.855 --> 00:22:05.457
This is just a raw egg.

00:22:05.457 --> 00:22:08.393
But now I add a bunch of syrup.

00:22:08.393 --> 00:22:11.496
Now, this is almost pure sugar.

00:22:11.496 --> 00:22:15.267
So the concentration
gradient is enormous,

00:22:15.267 --> 00:22:17.803
because the liquid
inside the egg

00:22:17.803 --> 00:22:19.438
doesn't have any sugar in it.

00:22:19.438 --> 00:22:22.741
But outside, the liquid has
huge amounts of sugar in it.

00:22:22.741 --> 00:22:25.210
So the water inside the
egg wants to diffuse out.

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:28.146
Eight hours, well,
it's kind of enough.

00:22:28.146 --> 00:22:31.083
18 hours, you pick
that membrane up.

00:22:31.083 --> 00:22:33.218
It's a really cool
membrane, the egg membrane.

00:22:33.218 --> 00:22:34.686
It's a semi-permeable membrane.

00:22:34.686 --> 00:22:35.754
And it's hard to see here.

00:22:35.754 --> 00:22:38.223
But you see, all the
water is kind of gone.

00:22:38.223 --> 00:22:40.492
It's totally deflated
but completely stable,

00:22:40.492 --> 00:22:42.427
because the membrane's
that strong.

00:22:42.427 --> 00:22:44.529
The water inside has left.

00:22:44.529 --> 00:22:46.698
And then if you
get some green dye

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:48.900
and you put it back
into some green water,

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.370
then you can make green eggs.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:53.572
And you can make
green ham that way.

00:22:53.572 --> 00:22:54.373
And that's osmosis.

00:22:57.676 --> 00:23:00.946
But it's semi permeable.

00:23:00.946 --> 00:23:02.848
You've got to give
the credit right.

00:23:02.848 --> 00:23:05.417
You got to give the credit
right in these things.

00:23:05.417 --> 00:23:08.353
This was years ago
when I thought I

00:23:08.353 --> 00:23:13.859
had a career in that direction.

00:23:13.859 --> 00:23:17.129
So the egg is just such a
cool membrane to play with.

00:23:17.129 --> 00:23:19.264
And I though, most of
us can access an egg.

00:23:19.264 --> 00:23:20.632
If you want to
play with a really

00:23:20.632 --> 00:23:23.835
cool semi-permeable
membrane, that is cool.

00:23:23.835 --> 00:23:28.106
And you really will see
Fick's first law at play.

00:23:28.106 --> 00:23:31.843
You will see it, and
you will feel it.

00:23:31.843 --> 00:23:35.213
Now, there's one thing
I want to comment

00:23:35.213 --> 00:23:36.715
on about all this stuff.

00:23:36.715 --> 00:23:41.820
And we are not going to
learn the details of entropy

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:42.487
in this class.

00:23:42.487 --> 00:23:47.259
That is really something
for a thermodynamics class.

00:23:47.259 --> 00:23:49.261
But I got to just
correct something,

00:23:49.261 --> 00:23:54.032
because it's in your textbook,
because a lot of this diffusion

00:23:54.032 --> 00:23:57.402
stuff, and in fact
dissolution-- remember we

00:23:57.402 --> 00:23:58.837
talked about salt dissolving?

00:23:58.837 --> 00:24:02.140
We did whole lectures on
this, the dissolution.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.677
Remember, these all have
to do with the total energy

00:24:05.677 --> 00:24:06.311
of the system.

00:24:09.347 --> 00:24:13.518
But we really focus in this
class on one part of that,

00:24:13.518 --> 00:24:17.556
on one part, which is called
the enthalpy, the bonding,

00:24:17.556 --> 00:24:18.757
the potential of the bonding.

00:24:18.757 --> 00:24:23.395
But there's a whole other part
that's related to the entropy.

00:24:23.395 --> 00:24:26.264
And we're not going to go into
this in detail, like I said.

00:24:26.264 --> 00:24:27.999
You won't be tested on this.

00:24:27.999 --> 00:24:30.168
But I got to bring it up.

00:24:30.168 --> 00:24:34.105
So entropy can be
really important.

00:24:34.105 --> 00:24:35.273
Why does something dissolve?

00:24:35.273 --> 00:24:38.643
It may be dominated by entropy.

00:24:38.643 --> 00:24:41.246
It may be dominated by entropy.

00:24:41.246 --> 00:24:43.482
That goes into the
total energy still.

00:24:43.482 --> 00:24:48.386
So we still have our happy
place, lower energy better.

00:24:48.386 --> 00:24:51.890
But as you learn more about
what that energy entails,

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:55.861
you've got to start including
entropy in the future.

00:24:55.861 --> 00:24:59.898
So Averill says, "...for now,
we can state that entropy is

00:24:59.898 --> 00:25:02.534
a thermodynamic property of all
substances that is proportional

00:25:02.534 --> 00:25:05.470
to their degree disorder."

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:07.839
No!

00:25:07.839 --> 00:25:10.675
That's not true.

00:25:10.675 --> 00:25:12.744
That is not true.

00:25:12.744 --> 00:25:14.513
So I have a question.

00:25:14.513 --> 00:25:17.182
So you see this all the time.

00:25:17.182 --> 00:25:20.352
Entropy, higher
entropy, more disorder.

00:25:20.352 --> 00:25:23.989
My room is messy, it's
got a high entropy.

00:25:23.989 --> 00:25:27.959
So which of these
has a higher entropy?

00:25:27.959 --> 00:25:33.398
Which one of these has a higher
entropy, and which is lower?

00:25:33.398 --> 00:25:35.033
This is a very simple grid.

00:25:35.033 --> 00:25:37.435
They've got the
same exact number.

00:25:37.435 --> 00:25:41.473
How many think that the
one on the right is--

00:25:41.473 --> 00:25:43.108
it's the smoother one, right?

00:25:43.108 --> 00:25:47.178
How many think that this is
the lower entropy system?

00:25:47.178 --> 00:25:48.613
I've set this up.

00:25:48.613 --> 00:25:51.283
I've set this up, because
you know that's not.

00:25:51.283 --> 00:25:53.051
But why?

00:25:53.051 --> 00:25:55.754
The reason is the real
reason for what entropy.

00:25:55.754 --> 00:26:00.225
It is not simply,
because in this-- these

00:26:00.225 --> 00:26:02.727
are computerized experiments.

00:26:02.727 --> 00:26:06.698
So we told a computer
to pick randomly a place

00:26:06.698 --> 00:26:09.200
on a grid a certain number
of times and then fill it in.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:12.103
But in this case,
you restricted it.

00:26:12.103 --> 00:26:12.837
You said, no.

00:26:12.837 --> 00:26:16.207
I can't have any two
squares next to each other.

00:26:16.207 --> 00:26:18.977
And in this case, you
simply did it randomly.

00:26:18.977 --> 00:26:21.212
So this looks smoother.

00:26:21.212 --> 00:26:25.116
This looks less disordered.

00:26:25.116 --> 00:26:27.752
But this has a higher entropy.

00:26:27.752 --> 00:26:28.320
Why?

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.622
Because you have fewer options.

00:26:30.622 --> 00:26:32.891
And that is what
entropy is about.

00:26:32.891 --> 00:26:37.896
Entropy is about the number of
possibilities that you have.

00:26:37.896 --> 00:26:41.366
Now, that might look more
disordered, it might not.

00:26:41.366 --> 00:26:44.569
But under the hood of
entropy is possibilities.

00:26:44.569 --> 00:26:48.974
It's number of states,
of accessible states.

00:26:48.974 --> 00:26:54.613
A salt atom in the crystal
has very few possibilities.

00:26:54.613 --> 00:26:56.915
If it were a perfect
crystal, one,

00:26:56.915 --> 00:27:00.285
it's got to be in the lattice.

00:27:00.285 --> 00:27:02.153
But once it goes
into the liquid,

00:27:02.153 --> 00:27:03.655
it's got many more
possibilities.

00:27:03.655 --> 00:27:07.025
It's those numbers of
states that matter.

00:27:07.025 --> 00:27:09.828
That's why its entropy
goes way, way up.

00:27:09.828 --> 00:27:11.429
It's the number
of possibilities,

00:27:11.429 --> 00:27:13.598
the number of possible states.

00:27:13.598 --> 00:27:14.432
OK, good.

00:27:14.432 --> 00:27:16.267
That was my aside on entropy.

00:27:16.267 --> 00:27:20.839
Now, we talked about
Fick's first law.

00:27:20.839 --> 00:27:22.007
We talked about diffusion.

00:27:22.007 --> 00:27:22.841
We looked at drops.

00:27:22.841 --> 00:27:26.578
And we made sure the gloves we
choose are going to be safe.

00:27:26.578 --> 00:27:28.313
What about solids?

00:27:28.313 --> 00:27:29.047
What about solids?

00:27:29.047 --> 00:27:32.250
Well, things diffuse
around in solids too.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:35.253
Here's a red atom that
is going to diffuse.

00:27:35.253 --> 00:27:37.489
Watch this super
high-tech video.

00:27:37.489 --> 00:27:38.256
How does it do it?

00:27:38.256 --> 00:27:39.658
A vacancy comes in.

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:43.828
Look at that vacancy, moving
around, moving around, bam!

00:27:43.828 --> 00:27:47.632
That atom moved, it
moved, self-diffusion.

00:27:47.632 --> 00:27:49.167
It moved.

00:27:49.167 --> 00:27:52.003
It's an atom of
the same type that

00:27:52.003 --> 00:27:55.473
was able to move in the lattice
because a vacancy diffused

00:27:55.473 --> 00:27:56.941
through right.

00:27:56.941 --> 00:27:58.576
And so there's the beginning.

00:27:58.576 --> 00:27:59.411
And there's the end.

00:27:59.411 --> 00:28:01.312
You see it moved
over by one position.

00:28:01.312 --> 00:28:03.048
But now we're all thinking.

00:28:03.048 --> 00:28:05.050
We're all thinking,
and we're going back

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:09.521
to our days of,
well, OK, hold on.

00:28:09.521 --> 00:28:11.589
What really happened?

00:28:11.589 --> 00:28:14.225
So here's the
vacancy on one side.

00:28:14.225 --> 00:28:15.927
Here, it went over to this side.

00:28:15.927 --> 00:28:18.697
There's the atom going through.

00:28:18.697 --> 00:28:20.031
So the vacancy moved that way.

00:28:20.031 --> 00:28:21.032
The atom moved that way.

00:28:21.032 --> 00:28:26.805
But as you know, that atom had
to experience some barrier.

00:28:26.805 --> 00:28:30.208
So it had to get
over some activation.

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:31.676
It had to get over
some activation.

00:28:31.676 --> 00:28:34.245
And that really helps us
answer a question that

00:28:34.245 --> 00:28:37.582
has been burning in our minds
this whole lecture, which is,

00:28:37.582 --> 00:28:41.720
what else does flux depend on.

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:45.123
We saw the concentration
gradient thing.

00:28:45.123 --> 00:28:47.258
We looked at it in
very simple terms.

00:28:47.258 --> 00:28:48.193
We understood it.

00:28:48.193 --> 00:28:51.096
But what about D itself?

00:28:51.096 --> 00:28:54.299
Well, when you see something
that's an activated process,

00:28:54.299 --> 00:28:57.769
there's only one person that
ever comes to mind, right?

00:28:57.769 --> 00:29:02.207
First name S, last
name A. And it's

00:29:02.207 --> 00:29:06.044
Svante, because you know that
if it's an activated process,

00:29:06.044 --> 00:29:08.213
then there's some
pre-exponential factor

00:29:08.213 --> 00:29:12.150
times the activation
energy divided by k B T

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:14.119
if it's for a single atom.

00:29:14.119 --> 00:29:17.422
Or if it's a mole, you use R.

00:29:17.422 --> 00:29:20.992
There's your activation
energy right there.

00:29:20.992 --> 00:29:23.228
But so now, we can
start connecting this.

00:29:23.228 --> 00:29:27.065
So one thing you know about
that diffusion constant is it

00:29:27.065 --> 00:29:30.168
will have a
temperature dependence.

00:29:30.168 --> 00:29:35.440
And you also know now that if
you plot the log of it versus 1

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:38.343
over T, you will get that.

00:29:38.343 --> 00:29:39.010
Oh, we're back.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:41.379
We're in our happy
place with this.

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:43.915
It's Svante.

00:29:43.915 --> 00:29:50.221
So this is an activated
process for an atom diffusing.

00:29:50.221 --> 00:29:54.926
These kind of atoms diffusing
through their own lattice,

00:29:54.926 --> 00:29:56.895
remember, these
have high barriers.

00:29:56.895 --> 00:29:58.263
This might be kind
of hard to do,

00:29:58.263 --> 00:30:00.198
but it's still going to happen.

00:30:00.198 --> 00:30:01.299
It's still going to happen.

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:02.934
You might also get
something like this.

00:30:02.934 --> 00:30:05.036
And this goes back
to our discussions

00:30:05.036 --> 00:30:07.071
of defects in crystals.

00:30:07.071 --> 00:30:12.744
You might get an
interstitial diffusion.

00:30:12.744 --> 00:30:15.747
So this is diffusion.

00:30:15.747 --> 00:30:18.583
This is the topic
that we're covering.

00:30:18.583 --> 00:30:21.519
But now we can
relate the equation

00:30:21.519 --> 00:30:26.257
that governs diffusion,
the D, to the crystal

00:30:26.257 --> 00:30:29.661
and to the chemistry, because
what is the barrier that that

00:30:29.661 --> 00:30:31.095
feels when it goes through.

00:30:31.095 --> 00:30:36.167
So remember, an interstitial,
like carbon and iron

00:30:36.167 --> 00:30:37.468
to make steel.

00:30:37.468 --> 00:30:42.540
That would be an interstitial
defect or an interstitial atom

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:46.678
that you've added to the
system, to the iron lattice.

00:30:46.678 --> 00:30:48.479
And how does it move?

00:30:48.479 --> 00:30:50.481
Well, that's
absolutely critical,

00:30:50.481 --> 00:30:52.417
because it tells
you how to make it.

00:30:52.417 --> 00:30:54.552
And we'll get to
an example of that.

00:30:54.552 --> 00:30:58.756
So if I look now
at these plots--

00:30:58.756 --> 00:31:01.626
and these are plots I think I
showed you back when we talked

00:31:01.626 --> 00:31:03.828
about interstitial defects.

00:31:03.828 --> 00:31:06.998
You can really connect
it to what we've learned.

00:31:06.998 --> 00:31:11.102
So just to start, these are
the log diffusion coefficients.

00:31:11.102 --> 00:31:12.570
So that's the
diffusion coefficient

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.539
that we're talking
about right there.

00:31:14.539 --> 00:31:18.009
Versus 1,000 over T-- so it's
1 over T scaled by 1,000.

00:31:18.009 --> 00:31:24.215
And these are those beautiful
Svante Arrhenius dependencies.

00:31:24.215 --> 00:31:26.251
And these are
experimental measurements.

00:31:26.251 --> 00:31:30.054
And look, here's
hydrogen in BCC iron.

00:31:30.054 --> 00:31:31.689
Here's hydrogen in FCC iron.

00:31:31.689 --> 00:31:36.394
Now, notice, at the
same temperature,

00:31:36.394 --> 00:31:37.862
there's a huge difference.

00:31:37.862 --> 00:31:42.300
And then, I have FCC
up here, and then I've

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:44.035
got BCC over there.

00:31:44.035 --> 00:31:44.535
Why?

00:31:44.535 --> 00:31:47.171
Well, those are the stable
phases at those temperatures.

00:31:47.171 --> 00:31:49.374
But notice that when they're
at the same temperature,

00:31:49.374 --> 00:31:50.275
there's a huge difference.

00:31:50.275 --> 00:31:50.775
Why?

00:31:50.775 --> 00:31:52.844
Because you know
from the crystal

00:31:52.844 --> 00:31:56.981
structure that
there's more room.

00:31:56.981 --> 00:32:03.154
So the activation barrier
will be lower in BCC.

00:32:03.154 --> 00:32:04.522
Those voids are bigger.

00:32:04.522 --> 00:32:05.290
There's more space.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:07.992
It's not as well packed.

00:32:07.992 --> 00:32:10.862
We've covered that.

00:32:10.862 --> 00:32:11.562
And look at this.

00:32:11.562 --> 00:32:13.231
So there's hydrogen,
it's really small.

00:32:13.231 --> 00:32:18.102
There's iron, iron in BCC Fe
compared to hydrogen in BCC Fe.

00:32:18.102 --> 00:32:19.837
Look at those
orders of magnitude.

00:32:19.837 --> 00:32:22.707
So there's an iron atom
diffusing in its own solid.

00:32:22.707 --> 00:32:23.775
And here's hydrogen atoms.

00:32:23.775 --> 00:32:26.077
You can see there's
carbon and so forth.

00:32:26.077 --> 00:32:29.213
So this is the picture
that comes back.

00:32:29.213 --> 00:32:31.382
If we want to think about
diffusion in solids,

00:32:31.382 --> 00:32:34.485
we can connect it to the
packing and crystal structures,

00:32:34.485 --> 00:32:37.288
because we know that these
atoms, these interstitial atoms

00:32:37.288 --> 00:32:41.526
are going to diffuse through
the interstitial space.

00:32:41.526 --> 00:32:43.027
What is the interstitial space?

00:32:43.027 --> 00:32:48.766
Well here, as just and example,
I showed just BCC and FCC.

00:32:48.766 --> 00:32:49.500
Here they are.

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:51.102
Here's is an octahedral site.

00:32:51.102 --> 00:32:53.071
This is BCC down here.

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:58.443
So notice, the shaded in atoms
are the lattice sites, BCC,

00:32:58.443 --> 00:33:01.012
at the corners
and in the middle.

00:33:01.012 --> 00:33:05.350
And now, you see, the red
atom is in the middle.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:06.651
Gesundheit.

00:33:06.651 --> 00:33:08.119
Let's say the red atom was here.

00:33:08.119 --> 00:33:09.153
And it's moving to there.

00:33:09.153 --> 00:33:10.655
And now it's in the middle.

00:33:10.655 --> 00:33:13.157
So it might take that
path as it diffuses.

00:33:13.157 --> 00:33:15.793
It's going to find the voids.

00:33:15.793 --> 00:33:18.129
Diffusing atoms find the voids.

00:33:18.129 --> 00:33:20.365
And so if you have something
that's trying to diffuse,

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:22.000
it's going to want
to find these voids.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:25.503
This is an octahedral site,
because this is an octahedron.

00:33:25.503 --> 00:33:28.039
And you can have one of those
in an FCC crystals as well.

00:33:28.039 --> 00:33:29.540
You can have an octahedral side.

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:33.478
So in FCC, you've got
lattice sites in the face

00:33:33.478 --> 00:33:34.912
but not in the center.

00:33:34.912 --> 00:33:38.750
And so its octahedral
site is here.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:39.951
You've got tetrahedral sites.

00:33:39.951 --> 00:33:44.088
Those are other voids
in the crystals.

00:33:44.088 --> 00:33:47.392
We touched on this a little bit
when we talked about defects

00:33:47.392 --> 00:33:48.026
in crystals.

00:33:48.026 --> 00:33:50.528
But now I want to
relate it to diffusion,

00:33:50.528 --> 00:34:00.238
because how a crystal has voids
is critical to D, how something

00:34:00.238 --> 00:34:01.906
diffuses in it,
whether that something

00:34:01.906 --> 00:34:03.307
is something in
the crystal itself

00:34:03.307 --> 00:34:06.411
or something you've added to it.

00:34:06.411 --> 00:34:10.915
And how it has voids depends
on the bonding, the chemistry,

00:34:10.915 --> 00:34:13.618
and the crystal structure.

00:34:13.618 --> 00:34:15.553
So you could answer
questions like this.

00:34:15.553 --> 00:34:22.326
These are some just fairly
straightforward questions

00:34:22.326 --> 00:34:23.226
that you could answer.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:30.234
What if I had carbon-- this
is what I just showed you--

00:34:30.234 --> 00:34:33.704
in alpha or gamma iron?

00:34:33.704 --> 00:34:35.940
Well, so I'll just
write the answer.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:41.179
It's alpha ion,
because it's an open--

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:42.647
I don't need to calculate stuff.

00:34:45.183 --> 00:34:48.018
Which one is BCC,
which one is FCC?

00:34:48.018 --> 00:34:51.522
And it's alpha, because
it's an open BCC structure.

00:34:51.522 --> 00:34:56.393
And so the diffusion barriers
are going to be lower.

00:34:56.393 --> 00:34:59.964
Iron in alpha iron
at 500 or 900?

00:34:59.964 --> 00:35:02.700
Well, now I've got a
temperature question.

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:09.073
I know D is going to be
higher, so 900 degrees C

00:35:09.073 --> 00:35:12.477
because of influence
of temperature.

00:35:12.477 --> 00:35:16.747
Iron or hydrogen in alpha iron.

00:35:16.747 --> 00:35:18.382
It's like what we
just talked about.

00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:20.885
So this would be particle size.

00:35:20.885 --> 00:35:22.787
H is smaller.

00:35:26.557 --> 00:35:30.828
These are conceptual questions.

00:35:30.828 --> 00:35:31.896
What's the fourth one?

00:35:31.896 --> 00:35:37.001
Silicon vacancy in silicon
or helium in silicon.

00:35:37.001 --> 00:35:40.304
Well, for a vacancy
to move, you need

00:35:40.304 --> 00:35:42.607
those kind of same atoms move.

00:35:42.607 --> 00:35:46.944
And if a same moving through
those sites, that's harder.

00:35:46.944 --> 00:35:51.449
That's harder than if a small
interstitial like hydrogen

00:35:51.449 --> 00:35:54.719
is moving through those voids.

00:35:54.719 --> 00:35:56.654
That's why, when
you look at this,

00:35:56.654 --> 00:36:00.491
iron is orders and orders
of magnitude slower.

00:36:00.491 --> 00:36:02.260
The D is orders and
orders of magnitude

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:07.498
slower than carbon or
hydrogen. And so that's

00:36:07.498 --> 00:36:12.703
going to be He, because the
vacancy diffusion is so much

00:36:12.703 --> 00:36:13.538
slower.

00:36:13.538 --> 00:36:16.240
And then five, Mg,
this is a good one,

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:20.545
because hydrogen in platinum
or hydrogen in magnesium.

00:36:20.545 --> 00:36:23.181
Well, magnesium has a
larger atomic radius.

00:36:31.656 --> 00:36:32.523
And you look it up.

00:36:32.523 --> 00:36:34.759
And you say, they've got the
same close-packed crystal

00:36:34.759 --> 00:36:35.960
structures.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:39.664
But one of them has
a larger radius.

00:36:39.664 --> 00:36:40.932
What does that mean?

00:36:40.932 --> 00:36:45.169
Well, it says something
about the voids as well.

00:36:45.169 --> 00:36:48.306
The voids are
going to be larger.

00:36:48.306 --> 00:36:51.309
Remember, this is happening
through the voids.

00:36:51.309 --> 00:36:52.843
This is happening
through the voids.

00:36:52.843 --> 00:36:56.881
This is how batteries
are engineered.

00:36:56.881 --> 00:37:02.420
This is why we have a
revolution in batteries.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:04.622
So this is a couple
of years old.

00:37:04.622 --> 00:37:06.424
There so many great
review articles

00:37:06.424 --> 00:37:08.226
on battery technologies.

00:37:12.964 --> 00:37:13.831
This is the cathode.

00:37:13.831 --> 00:37:15.066
By the way, what is a battery?

00:37:15.066 --> 00:37:20.137
Well, a lithium battery
is basically two sponges.

00:37:20.137 --> 00:37:24.508
And one of them
is usually carbon.

00:37:24.508 --> 00:37:26.043
And when you're
running your battery,

00:37:26.043 --> 00:37:28.879
lithium ions are going in.

00:37:28.879 --> 00:37:31.549
And the carbon is
so strong and able

00:37:31.549 --> 00:37:35.586
to hold those lithium
in and not break down.

00:37:35.586 --> 00:37:41.792
So we often use carbon
on the anode side.

00:37:41.792 --> 00:37:44.195
And then when we
charge it up, we

00:37:44.195 --> 00:37:46.030
push them back over to where?

00:37:46.030 --> 00:37:47.498
Where they came from.

00:37:47.498 --> 00:37:50.935
That's the cathode, which
is a lithium compound.

00:37:50.935 --> 00:37:55.539
But it has to be a material
that can hold lithium

00:37:55.539 --> 00:37:58.109
in it, hopefully at
very high concentration,

00:37:58.109 --> 00:38:03.781
but also be OK to lose it
and not completely collapse.

00:38:03.781 --> 00:38:06.984
And so you've got
lithium ion phosphate,

00:38:06.984 --> 00:38:08.352
those are these ones.

00:38:08.352 --> 00:38:10.221
You've got manganates.

00:38:10.221 --> 00:38:13.557
You've got cobalts,
cobalt oxide materials.

00:38:13.557 --> 00:38:17.495
There are so many
battery materials.

00:38:17.495 --> 00:38:19.530
They all work the same way.

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:21.799
If it's a lithium
battery, the lithium

00:38:21.799 --> 00:38:25.403
is going to leave some crystal
that has lithium in it.

00:38:25.403 --> 00:38:27.305
But the crystal has to stay.

00:38:27.305 --> 00:38:30.241
So it's like a sponge that
loses something from it

00:38:30.241 --> 00:38:31.242
but stays intact.

00:38:31.242 --> 00:38:33.844
And that's something
goes over to the anode.

00:38:33.844 --> 00:38:35.746
It gets soaked up there.

00:38:35.746 --> 00:38:39.050
And you can recharge,
discharge, recharge,

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:42.853
as you're just pushing these
lithiums back and forth.

00:38:42.853 --> 00:38:45.790
Oh, but you're pushing
them back and forth.

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:48.092
You are diffusing them.

00:38:48.092 --> 00:38:49.694
That's what you're doing.

00:38:49.694 --> 00:38:52.363
You are diffusing them
through whatever structure

00:38:52.363 --> 00:38:54.832
you're putting them into.

00:38:54.832 --> 00:38:57.868
So if it's the
cathode, and you're

00:38:57.868 --> 00:39:01.739
trying to get the lithium to
come back in or to go back out,

00:39:01.739 --> 00:39:05.376
then you can imagine that the
barriers and the dimensionality

00:39:05.376 --> 00:39:07.645
itself are crucial.

00:39:07.645 --> 00:39:10.281
What is an activation
energy for a lithium

00:39:10.281 --> 00:39:13.784
atom in a given material?

00:39:13.784 --> 00:39:15.653
And does it take a 1 D path?

00:39:15.653 --> 00:39:19.457
It turns out these can
be extremely efficient.

00:39:19.457 --> 00:39:20.057
Look at those.

00:39:20.057 --> 00:39:21.592
They're practically
holes, they're

00:39:21.592 --> 00:39:23.327
lines in the lithium
ion phosphate.

00:39:23.327 --> 00:39:25.396
It's a really nice
cathode material,

00:39:25.396 --> 00:39:26.831
because you've got
these channels.

00:39:26.831 --> 00:39:30.968
These high conduction
channels, these highways.

00:39:30.968 --> 00:39:34.071
But then you say, well,
but maybe I need 3D voids.

00:39:34.071 --> 00:39:35.473
Maybe it shouldn't
have channels.

00:39:35.473 --> 00:39:40.111
It should have voids, so
it can choose more paths.

00:39:40.111 --> 00:39:41.245
Maybe we have planes.

00:39:41.245 --> 00:39:43.647
Maybe it's a layered structure
where they have these 2D

00:39:43.647 --> 00:39:45.282
planes they can travel through.

00:39:45.282 --> 00:39:46.250
What's the best?

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:50.121
Well, it's not
trivial, because it's

00:39:50.121 --> 00:39:51.822
one of these, as
usual, constrained

00:39:51.822 --> 00:39:54.692
optimization problems, where,
when you make the pathway

00:39:54.692 --> 00:39:58.329
faster, you might need
to have more voids where

00:39:58.329 --> 00:40:00.531
you'll lose material.

00:40:00.531 --> 00:40:03.234
Or maybe you make the
material unstable.

00:40:03.234 --> 00:40:06.036
So you want it to have
a really high density.

00:40:08.572 --> 00:40:10.775
The lithium diffusion
and a battery material

00:40:10.775 --> 00:40:12.143
is related to the charge time.

00:40:15.479 --> 00:40:18.149
So here's another paper
from a few years ago.

00:40:18.149 --> 00:40:22.153
If you plot materials-- now,
here's the specific power.

00:40:22.153 --> 00:40:25.456
And so that's how much
power, watts per weight.

00:40:25.456 --> 00:40:26.991
Here's the watt hours.

00:40:26.991 --> 00:40:30.194
So that's the energy per weight.

00:40:30.194 --> 00:40:31.996
And this is the charge rate.

00:40:31.996 --> 00:40:33.397
This is the charge rate.

00:40:33.397 --> 00:40:37.201
And notice, so you want it to
be very, very fast-charging.

00:40:37.201 --> 00:40:38.436
And these are good materials.

00:40:38.436 --> 00:40:42.540
There's the lead-acid battery
that's still in your car.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:45.142
These are nickel metal
hydrides and so forth,

00:40:45.142 --> 00:40:46.177
other technologies.

00:40:46.177 --> 00:40:49.146
Here are the lithium
ion batteries.

00:40:49.146 --> 00:40:50.548
This is what's in
your cell phone.

00:40:50.548 --> 00:40:55.786
Notice something, as I get
up to faster charge rates,

00:40:55.786 --> 00:41:02.493
more open voids in material,
I lose energy density.

00:41:02.493 --> 00:41:04.195
I go this way.

00:41:04.195 --> 00:41:06.063
I don't want to go that way.

00:41:06.063 --> 00:41:08.365
I want to go this way.

00:41:08.365 --> 00:41:11.969
Constrained optimization,
these are the things.

00:41:11.969 --> 00:41:14.972
What goes into
solving these problems

00:41:14.972 --> 00:41:19.510
is calculating diffusion
barriers and energy densities

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:21.045
and all the other
things that matter.

00:41:21.045 --> 00:41:23.481
But if you want to
target fast charging,

00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:26.417
you've got to know the
diffusion barriers.

00:41:26.417 --> 00:41:29.787
One of the number one
challenges in battery design

00:41:29.787 --> 00:41:34.425
is to keep that high while
continuing to push up that way.

00:41:34.425 --> 00:41:37.795
Polymers, you could imagine
that a polymer is slower.

00:41:37.795 --> 00:41:39.897
It makes sense, right?

00:41:39.897 --> 00:41:41.799
Because it's
harder-- as we talked

00:41:41.799 --> 00:41:43.300
about when we talked
about polymers,

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:46.704
it's harder to make
crystalline polymers,

00:41:46.704 --> 00:41:50.674
to keep a polymer from
having some amorphous region.

00:41:50.674 --> 00:41:54.144
After all, you've got these
100,000-unit long strands

00:41:54.144 --> 00:41:56.113
of spaghetti.

00:41:56.113 --> 00:41:58.249
So it kind of makes
sense that the diffusion

00:41:58.249 --> 00:42:00.284
is going to be
lower in a polymer

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:03.587
than in one of these
crystals, like an olivine,

00:42:03.587 --> 00:42:05.022
lithium ion phosphate.

00:42:05.022 --> 00:42:06.023
So that makes sense.

00:42:06.023 --> 00:42:09.760
But polymers would be great,
because they're lighter.

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:12.596
And they can be flexible.

00:42:12.596 --> 00:42:14.765
And so we like polymer
batteries a lot.

00:42:14.765 --> 00:42:17.768
We want them for
many applications.

00:42:17.768 --> 00:42:20.137
So these are the kinds of
tradeoffs that you think about.

00:42:20.137 --> 00:42:23.240
And D is right there in the
center, because none of us

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:25.609
want a battery that
takes two days to charge.

00:42:28.212 --> 00:42:33.651
And now we get to the next
part, which I'll just introduce,

00:42:33.651 --> 00:42:37.187
which is, now we're going
to think about time.

00:42:37.187 --> 00:42:39.790
And I want to set
this problem up.

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:42.626
And then we'll solve it
on Wednesday and then

00:42:42.626 --> 00:42:44.395
and then finish.

00:42:44.395 --> 00:42:46.664
But I want to set it up,
because the next thing that's

00:42:46.664 --> 00:42:49.466
related to diffusion is time.

00:42:49.466 --> 00:42:51.035
And I want to give
you an example

00:42:51.035 --> 00:42:53.304
of how that's so
important with something

00:42:53.304 --> 00:42:55.940
called case hardening.

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:57.641
So if you buy a gear--

00:42:57.641 --> 00:43:00.477
and you do buy
gears all the time.

00:43:00.477 --> 00:43:03.280
Whether you like it or not or
know it or not, lots of stuff

00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:05.950
has gears in it.

00:43:05.950 --> 00:43:07.518
And you look at
the gear closely,

00:43:07.518 --> 00:43:09.186
it would look different
on the outside.

00:43:09.186 --> 00:43:12.356
If you do a
cross-section, most gears

00:43:12.356 --> 00:43:15.326
are going to look
different on the outside.

00:43:15.326 --> 00:43:18.529
And the reason has to do
with exactly what we just

00:43:18.529 --> 00:43:20.030
talked about.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:23.834
If this is a piece
of iron, pure iron,

00:43:23.834 --> 00:43:25.836
it's going to be too soft.

00:43:25.836 --> 00:43:27.037
So you're now turning this.

00:43:27.037 --> 00:43:31.742
And it's got to have
mechanical integrity.

00:43:31.742 --> 00:43:35.613
And so you've got
to make it harder.

00:43:35.613 --> 00:43:38.282
But the thing is, if you put
it in and it's pure iron,

00:43:38.282 --> 00:43:38.949
it's too soft.

00:43:38.949 --> 00:43:41.719
But now if you make it this
really ultra-high strength,

00:43:41.719 --> 00:43:46.490
carbon-infused steel,
it gets too brittle.

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:47.858
It gets too brittle.

00:43:47.858 --> 00:43:49.760
And so you need a balance.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:53.397
And one of the ways to balance
the mechanical strength

00:43:53.397 --> 00:43:57.067
of something like a gear or
many, many other materials

00:43:57.067 --> 00:43:58.969
is to harden just the outside.

00:43:58.969 --> 00:44:00.304
That's cold case hardening.

00:44:00.304 --> 00:44:02.506
The case, it's the case.

00:44:02.506 --> 00:44:03.641
How do you do it?

00:44:03.641 --> 00:44:05.576
Well, you get carbon.

00:44:05.576 --> 00:44:09.613
Remember, carbon going into
the iron makes it a lot harder,

00:44:09.613 --> 00:44:11.015
but it also makes
it more brittle.

00:44:11.015 --> 00:44:14.084
So if you made the whole
gear the same amount

00:44:14.084 --> 00:44:17.154
of carbon infused in iron,
is going to be too brittle.

00:44:17.154 --> 00:44:19.723
If you don't put any on, it's
not mechanically strong enough.

00:44:19.723 --> 00:44:23.260
So what you do is you
very, very delicately

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:27.665
expose this piece
of iron to carbon.

00:44:27.665 --> 00:44:30.567
You harden it, but
just on the outside.

00:44:30.567 --> 00:44:33.003
Now, how does the carbon get in?

00:44:33.003 --> 00:44:34.171
Diffusion.

00:44:34.171 --> 00:44:37.941
It gets into the material
through diffusion.

00:44:37.941 --> 00:44:41.445
So then the question at
your gear-making plant

00:44:41.445 --> 00:44:48.152
that you have to know
is, what temperature

00:44:48.152 --> 00:44:53.557
and how long should I expose
this gear to how much carbon.

00:44:53.557 --> 00:44:55.526
You've got concentration.

00:44:55.526 --> 00:44:56.794
You've got temperature.

00:44:56.794 --> 00:44:58.729
And you've got time.

00:44:58.729 --> 00:45:01.598
And if you know that,
you can actually

00:45:01.598 --> 00:45:03.867
process this material in
just the way you want.

00:45:03.867 --> 00:45:06.203
And you have to know
those parameters.

00:45:06.203 --> 00:45:10.107
And that is Fick's second law.

00:45:10.107 --> 00:45:14.244
So these are steel pipes.

00:45:14.244 --> 00:45:16.947
And they are going
to be case hardened.

00:45:16.947 --> 00:45:17.781
So what's happening?

00:45:17.781 --> 00:45:20.451
You've got an iron
with a certain amount

00:45:20.451 --> 00:45:22.519
of carbon content.

00:45:22.519 --> 00:45:26.056
But now, I need to add
more carbon but just

00:45:26.056 --> 00:45:31.895
the right amount to the
exterior of those steel pipes.

00:45:31.895 --> 00:45:33.130
How long should I do it?

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:36.100
And that is Fick's second
law, which I'll write down.

00:45:38.669 --> 00:45:40.571
So how do we figure that out?

00:45:40.571 --> 00:45:45.075
Well, we use the time dependent
version of Fick's law.

00:45:45.075 --> 00:45:57.254
And Fick's second law says that
the change in concentration

00:45:57.254 --> 00:46:02.960
with time is equal to the
diffusion constant times

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:09.099
the second derivative of the
concentration with position.

00:46:09.099 --> 00:46:10.534
That's Fick's second law.

00:46:10.534 --> 00:46:17.207
And so this is non-steady
state, which means--

00:46:17.207 --> 00:46:18.642
and we're not going
to derive this.

00:46:18.642 --> 00:46:21.411
But I do want you to know
that this is the Fick's

00:46:21.411 --> 00:46:23.680
second equation and
how you might apply it,

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:26.216
which is why we'll
do a problem with it.

00:46:26.216 --> 00:46:28.085
But it's non-steady state.

00:46:28.085 --> 00:46:34.057
And that means that
the concentration

00:46:34.057 --> 00:46:35.459
that you're going
to get is going

00:46:35.459 --> 00:46:39.730
to be a function of
position and time.

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:42.599
It's going to be a
function of both.

00:46:42.599 --> 00:46:45.002
So now, I can
answer the question.

00:46:45.002 --> 00:46:52.409
Remember before, I had to
keep the concentrations fixed

00:46:52.409 --> 00:46:54.945
to apply Fick's first law.

00:46:54.945 --> 00:46:57.047
Now I can answer the question.

00:46:57.047 --> 00:47:01.485
Well, OK, if I have
certain parameters,

00:47:01.485 --> 00:47:04.555
what is the concentration
going to be.

00:47:04.555 --> 00:47:06.089
What is the
concentration going to be

00:47:06.089 --> 00:47:08.525
at some time and some place?

00:47:08.525 --> 00:47:11.228
And that's exactly the
question I have to answer.

00:47:11.228 --> 00:47:16.533
How much carbon am I
going to have at what

00:47:16.533 --> 00:47:20.704
position in the case hardening?

00:47:20.704 --> 00:47:23.407
In what position of
the pipe exterior,

00:47:23.407 --> 00:47:25.642
how much carbon am I going
to have at what time?

00:47:25.642 --> 00:47:27.911
How long should I
leave it in the oven?

00:47:27.911 --> 00:47:30.080
That's the question
I'm trying to answer.

00:47:30.080 --> 00:47:32.749
And by the way,
what temperature?

00:47:32.749 --> 00:47:37.988
And so the thing that Fick's
second law helps us understand

00:47:37.988 --> 00:47:40.791
is, if I start with
some C S that's

00:47:40.791 --> 00:47:48.665
the starting concentration, and
I have a starting concentration

00:47:48.665 --> 00:47:56.240
C 0, and I expose at the surface
some higher concentration--

00:47:56.240 --> 00:47:59.209
remember, before,
I kept these fixed.

00:47:59.209 --> 00:47:59.977
I kept these fixed.

00:47:59.977 --> 00:48:01.245
And I got a flux.

00:48:01.245 --> 00:48:03.580
Now, I'm going to
be able to get this.

00:48:03.580 --> 00:48:09.052
So this line here
is time equals 0.

00:48:09.052 --> 00:48:14.558
But now I can actually
get this profile.

00:48:14.558 --> 00:48:24.101
Time 2, time 3, that's the
position through some surface.

00:48:24.101 --> 00:48:27.004
So this is what Fick's
second law tells us.

00:48:27.004 --> 00:48:31.575
It tells us these profiles, the
concentration in the material.

00:48:31.575 --> 00:48:36.313
And it just simply is a
time-dependent version

00:48:36.313 --> 00:48:37.080
of diffusion.

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:38.548
OK, what we're
going to do is we're

00:48:38.548 --> 00:48:40.117
going to pick up with this.

00:48:40.117 --> 00:48:42.486
We'll do this example
of case hardening.

00:48:42.486 --> 00:48:44.922
We'll do a few more
comments on the class.

00:48:44.922 --> 00:48:47.658
I'll tell you a little
bit about the final.

00:48:47.658 --> 00:48:51.528
And please bring your laptops
on Wednesday for the last 15

00:48:51.528 --> 00:48:52.029
minutes.

00:48:52.029 --> 00:48:55.499
We'll reserve for you
to do evaluations.

